course: dictionary
lesson: 9787
title: Surveys: asking and counting
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A survey is when you ask many people the same questions to find out what they think or do. To survey people is to ask them. Their answers are counted and shown in a tally, table or bar chart. The total number of people asked is the whole, and each answer group is a part of it.
explain: «A 2022 survey counted about 1,250 finless porpoises, slightly more than five years before.» Here survey means a careful count. Scientists looked carefully at the animals and counted them, so the number could be compared with the count from years earlier.
explain: To survey a place is to look at it carefully, or to measure land to make a map. A survey is also a careful look over something. «Surveys count about 8,700» rooms in the Forbidden City, which is careful counting, not a legend.
explain: A good survey asks fairly. «Reporters often pick dramatic people to interview; one angry man on TV doesn't show what most people think, but a fair survey can.» Ask yourself: did the survey ask enough people, and did it ask them all the same questions?
fact: A survey asks many people the same questions.
fact: Survey results are often shown in a tally, table, bar chart or pictogram.
fact: In a survey, the total number asked is the whole, and each answer group is a part.
fact: Land surveys measure land and are used to make maps.
fact: Surveys can count things too, such as the porpoises in a 2022 survey.
exercise: A class asks everyone which pet they like best and counts the answers. What is this called? | *A survey | A rumour | A legend | A puzzle
exercise: «A 2022 survey counted about 1,250 finless porpoises.» What does survey mean here? | *A careful count | A party | A tidy room | A race
exercise: Mum says, «We've had an offer, but it's subject to survey!» What is the survey probably? | A dance | A race to tidy rooms | *A careful look at the house | A cake
test: In a class survey of 20 children, 12 choose blue. What is the whole? | 12 | Blue | *20 | 8
test: Reporters pick one angry man on TV. Why is a fair survey better? | It is shorter and louder | It is always about a map | *It asks many people the same questions, so it shows what most think | It uses only dramatic people
exam: Which is NOT a meaning of survey? | To measure land to make a map | To look carefully at a place | To ask many people the same questions | *To eat a meal quickly
